  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Unseasonably cold temperatures will bring frost to high-\n  elevation valleys Monday morning, marking some of the coldest \n  temperatures seen so far this year. \n\n- A brief warm-up with dry conditions and low relative humidities\n  in the teens to low 20s is expected early next week. \n\n- A shortwave disturbance on Wednesday will increase thunderstorm activity\n  and winds, posing a near-critical fire weather risk in \n  southwest Montana before precipitation arrives.\n\n- An active, cooler weather pattern with above-normal precipitation\n  probabilities returns late next week. \n\nToday through Tuesday:\n\nClearing skies and drying air tonight will promote strong\nradiational cooling, leading to quite chilly Monday morning low \ntemperatures. These morning lows will be some of the coldest \ntemperatures we have seen so far this year. Widespread frost is \nexpected in high-elevation locations, including Seeley Lake, \nGeorgetown, Drummond to Butte, and Dixie Idaho, where temperatures\nwill drop into the upper 20s to low 30s. Localized light frost \nmay also reach lower elevation spots like Stevensville and the \nwestern Missoula Valley. \n\nTransitory ridging will bring warm, dry conditions Monday into\nTuesday, pushing valley highs back into the 80s. With this \nridge, a drier airmass will spread across the region, dropping \nafternoon relative humidities into the widespread teens to low 20 \npercent range on Monday. By Tuesday afternoon and evening, the \nridge will begin to break down, yielding a slight increase in \nwinds, scattered showers, and isolated thunderstorms. High cloud \nbases showers on Tuesday afternoon elevating the potential for \nstrong, gusty outflow winds.\n\nWednesday through Late Week:\n\nA notable shortwave disturbance will move through the area on\nWednesday, bringing a definite uptick in winds and more widespread\nthunderstorm activity, particularly across southwest Montana.\nBefore the precipitation arrives Wednesday afternoon, these\nstronger winds combined with the dry conditions could create near-\ncritical fire weather conditions in southwest Montana. As the\nsystem fully moves in, the showers and thunderstorms will carry\nthe potential to produce moderate to heavy rainfall. Wetting rain\nprobabilities will increase to 50-70% across much of the Northern\nRockies during this timeframe. \n\nLingering showers and elevated winds will persist into Thursday\nand Friday before winds taper off for the weekend. Looking into \nthe weekend, ensemble guidance shows strong confidence that a \ntrough of low pressure will sit over the Pacific Northwest. This \nwill maintain southwesterly flow over the Northern Rockies, \nkeeping temperatures slightly below seasonal averages and favoring\nan active regime with above-normal precipitation probabilities \ndue to daily shower and thunderstorm activity.",
